AUTHORS = [
A("Ralph Waldo Emerson", MAJ, "1803", "1882", "Boston, Massachusetts, United States",
  "American; Transcendentalist essay, lecture and poetry",
  "Essayist, lecturer and poet; the central figure of American Transcendentalism and the first American writer to make a philosophy of self-reliance a national literature.",
  "Born in Boston on 25 May 1803, the son of a Unitarian minister; he was educated at Harvard and at the Divinity School, was ordained in 1829, and resigned his Boston pastorate in 1832 over his refusal to administer the Lord's Supper. He travelled in Europe in 1833, met Carlyle, Coleridge and Wordsworth, and settled at Concord. Nature appeared anonymously in 1836; the Divinity School Address of 1838 made him notorious, and the Essays (1841, 1844) made him famous. He edited The Dial (1840-44), lectured across the northern states for forty years, and died at Concord on 27 April 1882.",
  "Emerson invented the American essay: informal, oracular, built of separate paragraphs that circle a subject rather than argue it through, and addressed to the individual reader's own intuitions. His subject is the divinity of the self and of nature, the rejection of institutions and the authority of the present moment, and the call to original work. He was the intellectual centre of the Concord circle and the man who made American writing independent of Europe.",
  "The aphoristic paragraph; abrupt transitions and serial assertion; the lecture as literary form; symbolic uses of nature; the first-person plural of address; images drawn from science, Plato and the Orient.",
  "Self-reliance and the divinity of the individual; nature as revelation; the rejection of institutional religion; the American scholar and the nation's intellectual independence; representative men and the uses of great persons; fate, compensation and the moral law.",
  "Emerson is the founding philosopher of American literature: 'Nature', 'Self-Reliance', 'The American Scholar' and the Divinity School Address created the vocabulary of American individualism, and his influence runs through Thoreau, Whitman, Dickinson, Nietzsche, William James and the whole tradition of American pragmatism.",
  infl=["Plato", "Immanuel Kant", "Samuel Taylor Coleridge", "Thomas Carlyle", "William Wordsworth", "Emanuel Swedenborg"],
  alt=["Waldo Emerson", "R. W. Emerson"],
  also=["Nature (1836; published anonymously)", "The American Scholar (address, 1837)",
        "An Address Delivered before the Senior Class in Divinity College, Cambridge (1838)",
        "Essays: First Series (1841)", "Essays: Second Series (1844)",
        "Poems (1847)", "Representative Men (1850)", "English Traits (1856)",
        "The Conduct of Life (1860)", "May-Day and Other Pieces (1867)",
        "Society and Solitude (1870)", "Letters and Social Aims (1876)",
        "The Journals of Ralph Waldo Emerson, 10 vols (1909-14, posthumous)",
        "The Early Lectures, 3 vols (1959-72, posthumous)"]),

A("Henry David Thoreau", MAJ, "1817", "1862", "Concord, Massachusetts, United States",
  "American; the nature essay, the journal and the political essay",
  "Essayist, naturalist and surveyor; the author of Walden and 'Civil Disobedience', the two founding texts of American nature writing and of the literature of conscience.",
  "Born at Concord, Massachusetts, on 12 July 1817; he was educated at Harvard and taught school, worked in his father's pencil factory, and from 1841 lived for periods in Emerson's household. He built his cabin at Walden Pond in 1845 and lived there two years, wrote 'Civil Disobedience' after spending a night in jail for refusing to pay a poll tax in 1846, and afterwards made several excursions to Maine, Cape Cod and Canada. He published A Week on the Concord and Merrimack Rivers (1849) and Walden (1854), and died of tuberculosis at Concord on 6 May 1862.",
  "Thoreau wrote the American sentence as a form of attention: his prose is exact, sinewy and full of puns, and his great subject is the disciplined perception of the natural world as a way of living rightly. Walden is at once a practical account of a two-year experiment in economy and a spiritual autobiography, and 'Civil Disobedience' is the founding modern argument that individual conscience outranks the state. His Journal, of over two million words, is the greatest American nature diary.",
  "Concrete, comma-heavy cumulative sentences; exact measurement and enumeration; pun, paradox and irony; the excursion narrative; the journal entry developed as an art form; quotations from classical and Eastern texts.",
  "Nature and the discipline of attention; simplicity and economic independence; civil disobedience and the individual against the state; slavery and the fugitive; the seasons and the local landscape; reading, the classics and self-culture.",
  "Thoreau is the founding figure of American nature writing and of the literature of non-violent resistance: Walden shaped the modern environmental movement, 'Civil Disobedience' influenced Tolstoy, Gandhi and Martin Luther King, and the Journal is one of the great unread works of the nineteenth century.",
  infl=["Ralph Waldo Emerson", "Charles Darwin", "Alexander von Humboldt", "William Bartram", "Gilbert White"],
  alt=["David Henry Thoreau"],
  also=["A Week on the Concord and Merrimack Rivers (1849)",
        "'Resistance to Civil Government' (1849; later 'Civil Disobedience')",
        "Walden; or, Life in the Woods (1854)", "'Slavery in Massachusetts' (1854)",
        "'A Plea for Captain John Brown' (1859)", "Excursions (1863, posthumous)",
        "The Maine Woods (1864, posthumous)", "Cape Cod (1865, posthumous)",
        "A Yankee in Canada (1866, posthumous)", "Letters to Various Persons (1865, posthumous)",
        "The Journal (1906, 14 vols, posthumous)"]),

A("Margaret Fuller", SEC, "1810", "1850", "Cambridgeport, Massachusetts, United States",
  "American; the Transcendentalist essay, criticism and the feminist treatise",
  "Critic, editor and feminist; the first woman to work as a professional literary critic in the United States and the author of Woman in the Nineteenth Century.",
  "Born Sarah Margaret Fuller at Cambridgeport, Massachusetts, on 23 May 1810, and educated by her father in the classics. She taught at Bronson Alcott's school, edited The Dial (1840-42), translated Goethe, and wrote for Horace Greeley's New-York Tribune from 1844, becoming the paper's first woman correspondent. In Europe she met Mazzini and aroused the admiration of the young Emerson circle; she married the Italian revolutionary Giovanni Angelo Ossoli in 1847 and supported the Roman Republic in 1849. She drowned with her husband and son when the ship carrying them to America was wrecked off Fire Island on 19 July 1850.",
  "Fuller wrote the most important American feminist argument before the Civil War: Woman in the Nineteenth Century turns the 'woman question' into a question of the self's right to develop, and it made her the first woman in America to command the authority of a public intellectual. Her criticism is the first substantial American body of writing on literature by a professional critic, and her dispatches from Italy are among the finest American war reportage of the century.",
  "Conversational, digressive and allusive prose; the critical survey; the dialogue and the 'Conversation' as form; quotation and translation; the impassioned direct address of the feminist argument.",
  "The right of women to self-development and to intellectual life; democracy and the 'greatness' of the modern era; American literature and national culture; European revolutions and Italian freedom; the dignity of the woman artist; Transcendentalist religion.",
  "Fuller is the founding figure of American feminism and the first professional woman of letters in the United States: Woman in the Nineteenth Century gave the American women's movement its first intellectual charter, and her Tribune criticism made literary journalism a serious American form. Her death off Fire Island made her a permanent figure of American legend.",
  infl=["Ralph Waldo Emerson", "Mary Wollstonecraft", "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe", "Bettina von Arnim", "George Sand", "Giuseppe Mazzini"],
  also=["Introduction to the Dial (1840-42)",
        "Summer on the Lakes, in 1843 (1844)", "Woman in the Nineteenth Century (1845)",
        "Papers on Literature and Art (1846)", "Memoirs of Margaret Fuller Ossoli (1852, posthumous)",
        "At Home and Abroad (1856, posthumous)", "Life Without and Life Within (1859, posthumous)",
        "These Sad but Glorious Days: Dispatches from Europe, 1846-1850 (1992, posthumous)"]),

A("Nathaniel Hawthorne", MAJ, "1804", "1864", "Salem, Massachusetts, United States",
  "American; the romance, the tale and the literature of the Puritan past",
  "Novelist and short-story writer; the first great American novelist and the master of the American romance.",
  "Born in Salem, Massachusetts, on 4 July 1804, into a family descended from a judge of the 1692 witch trials; he was educated at Bowdoin College, where his friends were Longfellow and Franklin Pierce. He published Twice-Told Tales in 1837 after twelve years of solitary apprenticeship, worked in the Boston customs house and the Salem custom house, and published The Scarlet Letter in 1850, followed by The House of the Seven Gables (1851), The Blithedale Romance (1852) and The Marble Faun (1860). He was American consul at Liverpool in 1853-57 and died at Plymouth, New Hampshire, on 19 May 1864.",
  "Hawthorne wrote the American romance as an enquiry into guilt: his subject is the inward consequence of sin, the secret life of the respectable, and the relation of the present to a Puritan past that will not let it go. His method is allegory handled with great psychological delicacy — the scarlet letter, the birthmark, the house of seven gables — and his prose is a cool, deliberate, faintly ironic instrument for the analysis of conscience.",
  "Allegory with psychological depth; the single emblem around which a story is built; the romance of the 'neutral territory' between the real and the imaginary; the narrator's ironic commentary; the long retrospective sentence; the New England past as a haunting.",
  "Sin, guilt and the conscience; the Puritan inheritance and the witch trials; secrecy and the divided life; the artist and the community; adultery and the position of women; the American past and the failure of utopia.",
  "Hawthorne is the first major American novelist and the central writer of the American romance: The Scarlet Letter is the founding novel of American literature, his tales created the American short story of moral mystery, and his influence runs through Melville, James, Faulkner and O'Connor.",
  infl=["Edmund Spenser", "John Bunyan", "Sir Walter Scott", "William Shakespeare", "Jean-Jacques Rousseau"],
  also=["Fanshawe (1828)", "Twice-Told Tales (1837)", "Grandfather's Chair (1841)",
        "Mosses from an Old Manse (1846)", "The Snow-Image and Other Twice-Told Tales (1851)",
        "The Scarlet Letter (1850)", "The House of the Seven Gables (1851)",
        "A Wonder-Book for Girls and Boys (1852)", "The Blithedale Romance (1852)",
        "Tanglewood Tales (1853)", "Life of Franklin Pierce (1852)",
        "The Marble Faun (1860)", "Our Old Home (1863)",
        "Septimius Felton (unfinished, 1872, posthumous)",
        "The Dolliver Romance (unfinished, 1876, posthumous)"]),

A("Herman Melville", MAJ, "1819", "1891", "New York City, United States",
  "American; the epic romance, the sea narrative and the short story",
  "Novelist, poet and short-story writer; the author of Moby-Dick and the greatest American writer of the sea.",
  "Born in New York City on 1 August 1819; his father's bankruptcy and death in 1832 ended his schooling, and he went to sea in 1839 on a merchant ship and in 1841 on the whaler Acushnet, from which he deserted in the Marquesas in 1842. Typee (1846) and Omoo (1847) made him famous as a writer of the South Seas; Moby-Dick (1851) failed, Pierre (1852) scandalised, and after The Confidence-Man (1857) he wrote almost nothing but poetry and the posthumous Billy Budd. He worked as a customs inspector in New York from 1866 and died there on 28 September 1891.",
  "Melville wrote the American epic against the American romance: Moby-Dick is at once a whaling narrative, an encyclopaedia of cetology, a tragedy of obsession and a metaphysical argument, and its prose is the most various in nineteenth-century American writing — Shakespearean soliloquy, comic catalogue, sermon, stage direction and lyric. His later work turns to the short story and to poetry, and 'Bartleby' and Billy Budd are among the most haunting things in the language.",
  "Encyclopaedic digression and catalogue; Shakespearean soliloquy and aside; multiple narrative genres inside one book; symbol and emblem pushed to the point of allegory; alliteration and blank-verse cadence in prose; irony and black comedy.",
  "The whale and the sea as metaphysical absolutes; obsession and the will; race, slavery and the crew of the Pequod; the failure of American democracy; the artist and a hostile market; innocence and the law ('Bartleby', Billy Budd); the Pacific and the colonial encounter.",
  "Melville is the greatest of American writers and Moby-Dick the central American book: unread in his lifetime, rediscovered in the 1920s, it is now the foundational text of American studies and of the criticism of empire, race and the sublime. Billy Budd and 'Bartleby' are permanent parts of the world's short fiction.",
  infl=["William Shakespeare", "John Milton", "Thomas Carlyle", "Nathaniel Hawthorne", "Laurence Sterne", "The Bible (Authorised Version)"],
  alt=["Herman Melvill"],
  also=["Typee: A Peep at Polynesian Life (1846)", "Omoo: A Narrative of Adventures in the South Seas (1847)",
        "Mardi: and a Voyage Thither (1849)", "Redburn: His First Voyage (1849)",
        "White-Jacket; or, The World in a Man-of-War (1850)", "Moby-Dick; or, The Whale (1851)",
        "Pierre; or, The Ambiguities (1852)", "Israel Potter: His Fifty Years of Exile (1855)",
        "The Piazza Tales (1856: 'Bartleby', 'Benito Cereno', 'The Encantadas')",
        "The Confidence-Man: His Masquerade (1857)", "Battle-Pieces and Aspects of the War (1866)",
        "Clarel: A Poem and Pilgrimage in the Holy Land (1876)",
        "John Marr and Other Sailors (1888)", "Timoleon (1891)",
        "Billy Budd, Sailor (unfinished; 1924, posthumous)"]),

A("Walt Whitman", MAJ, "1819", "1892", "West Hills, Long Island, New York, United States",
  "American; the free-verse epic and the democratic lyric",
  "Poet, journalist and essayist; the inventor of American free verse and the poet of American democracy.",
  "Born at West Hills, Long Island, on 31 May 1819; he left school at eleven, worked as a printer's devil, a schoolmaster and a journalist, and edited the Brooklyn Eagle (1846-48) and the New Orleans Crescent (1848). He set the type for the first edition of Leaves of Grass himself in 1855; Emerson's letter of praise made him known. During the Civil War he nursed wounded soldiers in Washington hospitals, and after a stroke in 1873 he lived in Camden, New Jersey. He died there on 26 March 1892.",
  "Whitman invented a poetry for the American continent: long unrhymed lines generated by the breath and by the catalogue, an inclusive vocabulary that admits the technical, the vulgar and the democratic, and a first-person speaker who is at once an individual body and a national 'I'. Leaves of Grass grew through nine editions in his lifetime and is the founding book of modern American poetry.",
  "Free verse built on syntactical parallelism and anaphora; catalogue and inventory; the long breath-line; first-person address to the reader; colloquial and technical diction; the poem as a growing organism revised across a lifetime.",
  "Democracy, equality and 'the common man'; the body, sex and physical life; death and immortality; the Civil War and Lincoln; comradeship and the love of men; America as the future of the race; the poet as national voice.",
  "Whitman is the founder of modern American poetry and one of the most influential poets in the world: Leaves of Grass changed the possibilities of verse in English, his Civil War poems are the finest written by an American, and his influence runs through Hopkins, Crane, Hart Crane, Neruda, Ginsberg and the Beat generation.",
  infl=["Ralph Waldo Emerson", "The Bible (Authorised Version)", "Homer", "William Shakespeare", "Giambattista Vico", "George Sand"],
  also=["Franklin Evans; or, The Inebriate (novel, 1842)",
        "Leaves of Grass (1855; nine editions to 1891-92)",
        "Drum-Taps (1865)", "Sequel to Drum-Taps (1865-66, including 'When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom'd')",
        "Democratic Vistas (1871)", "Passage to India (1871)",
        "As a Strong Bird on Pinions Free (1872)", "Memoranda During the War (1875-76)",
        "Two Rivulets (1876)", "Specimen Days (1882)",
        "November Boughs (1888)", "Good-Bye My Fancy (1891)",
        "The Complete Prose Works (1892)", "The Collected Writings (1961-, posthumous)"]),

A("Emily Dickinson", MAJ, "1830", "1886", "Amherst, Massachusetts, United States",
  "American; the lyric, the riddle and the poetry of the inner life",
  "Poet; one of the two or three greatest lyric poets in English, and the central woman poet of the nineteenth century.",
  "Born in Amherst, Massachusetts, on 10 December 1830, the daughter of a lawyer and congressman; she was educated at Amherst Academy and Mount Holyoke Female Seminary, which she left after a year. She lived almost entirely in her father's house, increasingly withdrawn after 1861, dressed in white, and conducted her closest relationships by letter. She wrote nearly 1,800 poems, sewn into fascicles, and published fewer than a dozen in her lifetime. She died in Amherst on 15 May 1886; the first collection appeared in 1890.",
  "Dickinson wrote the lyric of extreme compression: her poems are short, usually rhymed in ballad or hymn stanzas, and organised around a single image or a riddle that the reader must solve. She writes about death, immortality, the mind, nature, love and fame with a wit and a metaphysical daring unmatched in American poetry, and her dashes, capitals and slant rhymes create a syntax of hesitation that is entirely her own.",
  "Hymn and ballad measures broken by slant rhyme and irregular stress; dashes in place of punctuation; capitalised nouns; riddling circumlocution for the central subject; paradox and irony; the poem as a single compressed argument.",
  "Death and immortality; the mind and its power; nature and the seasons; love, renunciation and absence; religious doubt and the Calvinist inheritance; fame, publication and the woman artist.",
  "Dickinson is one of the greatest poets in English and the founder of a distinctively American lyric of the inward life: her influence on twentieth-century poetry is as large as Whitman's, and the recovery of her manuscripts has made her a central figure in the criticism of gender, religion and American poetics.",
  infl=["Ralph Waldo Emerson", "Isaac Watts", "William Shakespeare", "John Keats", "Elizabeth Barrett Browning", "Charlotte Brontë"],
  alt=["Emily Elizabeth Dickinson"],
  also=["Poems by Emily Dickinson (1890, posthumous, ed. Higginson and Todd)",
        "Poems, Second Series (1891, posthumous)", "Poems, Third Series (1896, posthumous)",
        "The Single Hound (1914, posthumous)", "Further Poems (1929, posthumous)",
        "Bolts of Melody (1945, posthumous)", "The Poems of Emily Dickinson, 3 vols (1955, ed. Johnson)",
        "The Manuscript Books of Emily Dickinson (1981)", "The Letters (1958, 3 vols)",
        "The Gorgeous Nothings (facsimile fascicles, 2013)"]),

A("Harriet Beecher Stowe", MAJ, "1811", "1896", "Litchfield, Connecticut, United States",
  "American; the anti-slavery novel and the regional sketch",
  "Novelist and abolitionist; the author of Uncle Tom's Cabin, the most influential novel ever published in the United States.",
  "Born at Litchfield, Connecticut, on 14 June 1811, the daughter of the evangelical reformer Lyman Beecher; she was educated at her sister Catharine's Hartford Female Seminary and moved with the family to Cincinnati in 1832, where she married the clergyman and biblical scholar Calvin Stowe in 1836 and lived for eighteen years on the border of the slave state of Kentucky. Uncle Tom's Cabin was serialised in 1851-52 and published as a book in 1852; she wrote some thirty more books and died at Hartford on 1 July 1896.",
  "Stowe wrote the novel as a moral instrument: Uncle Tom's Cabin is built for conversion, moving from comic domesticity to scenes of almost unbearable suffering, and its sentimental method was the most effective political rhetoric of the century. She was also a superb regional writer, and her New England village sketches and her novels of Calvinist households are the finest fictional record of nineteenth-century New England domestic life.",
  "Sentimental tableaux designed to produce tears and conversion; dialect speech, black and Yankee; direct address to the reader; comic domestic dialogue; scriptural quotation and hymn; abrupt shifts of tone from comedy to martyrdom.",
  "Slavery and its destruction of families; Christian faith under persecution; the moral responsibility of the North; domesticity and the home as a moral unit; Calvinism and conversion; women's moral authority.",
  "Uncle Tom's Cabin is the most influential novel in American history: it sold 300,000 copies in its first year in the United States and a million in Britain, was translated into more than sixty languages, and is credited by Lincoln's reported remark with starting the Civil War. It is a founding text of the American novel, of abolitionist literature and of the literature of human rights.",
  infl=["Lyman Beecher", "Catharine Esther Beecher", "John Bunyan", "Charles Dickens", "Walter Scott", "Harriet Martineau"],
  alt=["Harriet Elizabeth Beecher Stowe"],
  also=["The Mayflower; or, Sketches of Scenes and Characters among the Descendants of the Pilgrims (1843)",
        "Uncle Tom's Cabin; or, Life among the Lowly (1852)",
        "A Key to Uncle Tom's Cabin (1853)", "Sunny Memories of Foreign Lands (1854)",
        "Dred: A Tale of the Great Dismal Swamp (1856)", "The Minister's Wooing (1859)",
        "The Pearl of Orr's Island (1862)", "Agnes of Sorrento (1862)",
        "Oldtown Folks (1869)", "Pink and White Tyranny (1871)",
        "My Wife and I (1871)", "Palmetto-Leaves (1873)",
        "Uncle Tom's Cabin on the American Stage (adaptations, 1850s)",
        "Poganuc People: Their Loves and Lives (1878)"]),

A("Frederick Douglass", MAJ, "1818", "1895", "Cordova, Talbot County, Maryland, United States",
  "African-American; the slave narrative, the oration and the political essay",
  "Orator, editor and autobiographer; the greatest African-American writer of the nineteenth century and the most eloquent voice of the abolitionist movement.",
  "Born Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey in Talbot County, Maryland, in February 1818; he was enslaved until 1838, when he escaped to New York disguised as a sailor, and took the name Douglass. He spoke for the Massachusetts Anti-Slavery Society from 1841, published his Narrative in 1845, founded and edited the North Star in Rochester (1847-51), and after the Civil War held federal appointments in Haiti and Washington. He wrote three autobiographies and died in Washington on 20 February 1895.",
  "Douglass wrote the American language of freedom: his Narrative is the classic slave narrative, built on the movement from ignorance to literacy to liberty, and his oratory — the Fourth of July address of 1852, 'What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?' — is the greatest American political speech of the century. His prose is lucid, cadenced and controlled, and it turns the language of the Declaration against the republic that betrayed it.",
  "First-person narrative of escape and self-making; direct oratorical address; biblical and constitutional quotation turned to indictment; irony and sarcasm; exact documentary detail of slavery's economy.",
  "Slavery and the destruction of the self; literacy and the awakening of freedom; the hypocrisy of American democracy; the Constitution and natural rights; Reconstruction and the failure of emancipation; the position of black women.",
  "Douglass is the central African-American writer of the nineteenth century and one of the great prose writers in the language: his Narrative is the founding text of the African-American literary tradition, his Fourth of July oration is a permanent indictment of American hypocrisy, and his life made him the most photographed and most widely heard black man of his century.",
  infl=["The Bible (Authorised Version)", "William Lloyd Garrison", "Daniel O'Connell", "Thomas Paine", "Hugh Auld (his Baltimore teacher)"],
  alt=["Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey"],
  also=["Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave (1845)",
        "The North Star (editor, 1847-51)", "Frederick Douglass' Paper (editor, 1851-60)",
        "'What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?' (oration, 1852)",
        "My Bondage and My Freedom (1855)",
        "Life and Times of Frederick Douglass (1881; revised 1892)",
        "The Heroic Slave (novella, 1853)",
        "Speeches, debates and editorials (1841-94)"]),

A("Harriet Jacobs", SEC, "1813", "1897", "Edenton, North Carolina, United States",
  "African-American; the slave narrative and the literature of the fugitive woman",
  "Writer and abolitionist; the author of Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl, the first full-length slave narrative written by a woman.",
  "Born into slavery in Edenton, North Carolina, in 1813; she was taught to read by her mistress and, after her mother's death, was taken into the household of Dr James Norcom, whose sexual pursuit she escaped by hiding for seven years in a small garret above her grandmother's shed. She escaped to the North in 1842, was freed in 1852, worked as a nursemaid in New York and as a relief worker among the freedpeople in Virginia during and after the Civil War. She died in Washington in March 1897.",
  "Jacobs wrote the slave narrative as a woman's testimony: Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl tells what the male narratives could not — the sexual exploitation of enslaved women, the destruction of motherhood, and the cost of protecting a daughter's honour — and it does so in a controlled, ironic first person that addresses a white female readership directly. It is the founding text of African-American women's autobiography.",
  "First-person retrospective narrative with direct appeal to the reader; the concealment of the narrator's name and identity; scenes of domestic terror and comic endurance; the seven-year hiding as a central emblem; plain documentary prose with a strong ironic edge.",
  "Slavery and sexual exploitation; motherhood and the sale of children; the cult of true womanhood and its exclusion of black women; concealment, literacy and self-defence; the North and the Fugitive Slave Law; Christian hypocrisy.",
  "Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl is a founding text of African-American women's writing and of American autobiography: it is the first slave narrative to make the sexual abuse of enslaved women its central subject, and its account of the seven-year hiding is one of the most extraordinary documents in American literature. Long read as fiction, it was authenticated as autobiography in the 1980s and is now canonical.",
  infl=["The Bible (Authorised Version)", "Frederick Douglass", "Lydia Maria Child", "Harriet Beecher Stowe", "Olaudah Equiano"],
  alt=["Linda Brent"],
  also=["Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl, Written by Herself (1861, as Linda Brent; ed. Lydia Maria Child)",
        "Letters and relief work among the freedpeople (1862-68, in the American Freedman)"]),
]
